literature/ssh-ground-truth.md:144:### State-machine behaviour Terrapin relies on
literature/ssh-ground-truth.md:146:- **RcvIncrease**: a MitM raises the peer's receive sequence number by N by injecting N `IGNORE` messages, or any other message that gets no response, during the unencrypted handshake ([Terrapin, §4.1, Fig. 4a](https://www.usenix.org/system/files/usenixsecurity24-baumer.pdf)).
literature/ssh-ground-truth.md:148:- **SndIncrease/SndDecrease**: an unrecognised message ID during the handshake makes the peer send `UNIMPLEMENTED`, which raises its send counter; the MitM drops those replies ([Terrapin, §4.1, Figs. 4c, 4d](https://www.usenix.org/system/files/usenixsecurity24-baumer.pdf)).
literature/ssh-ground-truth.md:154:- Prefix truncation: the MitM injects IGNOREs before NEWKEYS and deletes the same number of messages from the start of the secure channel, typically the server's `EXT_INFO` ([Terrapin, §4.2, §5.2](https://www.usenix.org/system/files/usenixsecurity24-baumer.pdf)).
literature/ssh-ground-truth.md:155:  This is exploitable with ChaCha20-Poly1305 and CBC-EtM, not with AES-CTR plus HMAC (our mapper's cipher) ([Terrapin, Table 1](https://www.usenix.org/system/files/usenixsecurity24-baumer.pdf)).
literature/ssh-ground-truth.md:156:  So our learner cannot run Terrapin itself, but it can see whether its precondition holds: optional messages tolerated during the initial KEX.
